his till's, any sales in the outbox not yet counted, and imported Square history), the customers, the deals and the catalogue names. They are handed nothing that can write, and a test freezes the snapshot to prove they do not change it. Money is returned as text such as $12.50 so a small model never reads cents. Each tool declares a field map that puts every field it can return into a privacy category, and the data gate removes or masks what the endpoint may not receive. A tool also never matches on something the model may not read: a customer is not found by name when names are hidden, and a gift card is not found by code when codes are hidden, because a match would tell the model who bought what. When names and emails are both hidden, a customer search returns an empty list and the privacy note, exactly as it does for a customer who does not exist. How an order was paid is treated as a payment detail and is never sent. Card details are never put in a result at all. On practice data the snapshot is empty, which is a second lock behind the practice mode block. Code: `src/lib/assistantData.ts` (the queries, tested), `src/lib/assistantDataTools.ts` (the tool definitions and field maps), `src/lib/assistantDataIO.ts` (the snapshot).\n\n### How the screen context works\nThe chat screen reads the navigation and your display mode once, at the moment you send a question (`registerContext` in `src/lib/assistantContext.ts`), and the `screen_context` tool hands that snapshot back. The tool never reads the app itself and has nothing that can write. The last pages on the navigation stack are Help and the chat, so they are skipped to find the page you were on (`standingOn`), and the app graph names it (`screenOfRoute` in `src/lib/appGraph.ts`, the same route table training uses). A page the graph does not know is reported as one it cannot name, and the tab under it is not used in its place, because that would say the wrong screen. The page is looked up among the screens your role